6.77.m:
Having placed Śrīdhara on the tuft of hair, Śrīkara below the tuft, / Hṛṣīkeśa on the hair, and supreme Nārāyaṇa on the crown of the head,

one should place Viṣṇu on the upper ear, and the One who sleeps on the waters on the forehead; / having placed Vibhu on the pair of eyebrows, and Hari between the eyebrows,

Narasimha on the tip of the nose, the One who rests in the ocean on the ears, / the Lotus-Eyed on the eyes, one should place the Bearer of the Earth beneath them;

Kalkinātha on the cheeks, Vāmana at the root of the ears, / placing the Conch-Bearer on the temples, and Govinda on the mouth;

Mukunda on the rows of teeth, and the Lord of Speech on the tongue; / placing Rāma on the chin, and Vaikuṇṭha on the neck;

the Slayer of Bala below the armpits, and the Slayer of Kaṃsa on the shoulders; / placing Aja on the two arms, and the Wielder of the Bow Śārṅga on the two hands;

Saṃkarṣaṇa on the thumb, the Cowherd on the rows of fingers; / having placed Adhokṣaja on the chest, and Śrīvatsa in its center;

Aniruddha on the breasts, and Dāmodara on the belly; / Padmanābha on the navel, and Keśava below the navel;

the god Bearer of the Earth on the penis, and the Elder Brother of Gada on the anus; / the Wearer of Yellow Garments on the waist, and the Enemy of Madhu on the pair of thighs;

the Enemy of Mura on the calves, Janārdana on the pair of knees; / placing the Lord of Serpents on the ankles, and Trivikrama on the feet;

the Husband of Śrī on the big toe, and the Bearer of the Earth below the feet; / in all the pores of the body, the wise man should place Viṣvaksena;

placing Matsya in the flesh, one should place Kūrma in the fat, / the Boar in the marrow, and Acyuta in all the bones;

the One Dear to the Twice-Born in the bone marrow, the Lord of the White in the semen, / the Sacrifice-Person in the entire body, and the Supreme Self in the self.

Having performed the ritual placement in this way, he becomes Nārāyaṇa visibly; / so long as he does not speak anything, for that long he remains identical with Viṣṇu.

Having taken pure kuśa grass with root and tips, concentrated, / he who makes peace should wipe all limbs here with the tips of the kuśa grass.

A devotee of Viṣṇu especially, when someone is tormented by disease, planetary spirits, or poison, / should perform this auspicious rite of appeasement for the sick and those afflicted by poison.

By that, O brāhmaṇa, destruction of all diseases is brought about. / “Homage to the glorious supreme reality, the noble Puruṣa,

the formless, multiform, all-pervading Supreme Self, / the Boar, the Man-Lion, and the Dwarf, who bestows happiness!”
